<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizhny Novgorod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1221">1221</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Gorky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nizhny Novgorod is a city at the confluence of the Oka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, founded in 1221 by Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-of-vladimir">Yuri II of Vladimir</a>. In the 14th century it was the capital of the Grand Principality of Nizhny Novgorod-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> before passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1392. It later became a great trading centre and was called Gorky from 1932 to 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod.jpg" alt="Nizhny Novgorod" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexTref871 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nizhny_Novgorod_2025-04-29_Minin_and_Pozharsky_square_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its princes 'gathered the Russian lands' by purchase, inheritance, marriage, diplomacy and war, absorbing <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> in 1392, Yaroslavl in 1463, Rostov in 1474, the Novgorod Republic in 1478, Tver in 1485, Vyatka in 1489, Pskov in 1510 and Ryazan around 1521.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Grand Duchy of Moscow, Tver, <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> and other principalities of north-eastern Russia were all formed from its appanages, and the Moscow princes counted their descent from Vsevolod the Big Nest through Alexander Nevsky.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1221 Grand Prince Yuri II founded <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> as a forward base against them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The composer Mily Balakirev wrote the song down from Nikolay Aleynikov in <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> in 1860 or 1861, and published it with his own arrangement in his collection of Russian folk songs in 1866.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li><li><blockquote>Meine said the idea came to him one summer night as he sat at the <b>Gorky</b> Park centre looking at the Moskva River, and called the song his personal reappraisal of what had happened in the world in recent years.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wind-of-change">Wind of Change</a></li><li><blockquote>The alliance joined the Pereyaslavl, Yaroslavl, Yuryev, <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> and Murom principalities.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
